Subject: [PATCH] ao_mediafoundation: play E-AC-3 through the system decoder

FFmpeg decodes only the 5.1/7.1 bed of a Dolby Atmos (JOC) E-AC-3 track, and
ao_wasapi can pass the bitstream on only in exclusive mode, to a device that
decodes it. Headphones and built-in speakers with a Windows spatial sound
format (Dolby Atmos for Headphones, ...) therefore never get the objects.

This AO takes the E-AC-3 access units back out of the IEC 61937 stream
ad_spdif produces and serves them to a Media Foundation session from a media
source of its own. The topology loader inserts the Dolby decoder MFT, which
negotiates MFAudioFormat_Float_SpatialObjects with the audio renderer. The
session's presentation clock is the audio clock.

ao_wasapi redirects E-AC-3 to it when the device refuses the bitstream
(ao->redirect, as ao_coreaudio does for coreaudio_exclusive), together with the
device it was opened for, which the AO resolves as ao_wasapi does. So a device
that takes the bitstream keeps getting it, a device picked with
--audio-device=wasapi/... is used, and the AO is neither probed on its own nor
listed in audio-device-list. It refuses to initialize when the decoder would not
output objects (no spatial format on the endpoint, no decoder), and mpv decodes
the track as before.

Once playing, a failure asks the player to decode the track instead
(ao_request_pcm_fallback(), 0244): an error from the session, a Start or resume
the session refuses, no MESessionStarted within 3 s, or a clock that stands
still for 3 s while the renderer has everything it asked for. A reload would
reopen the same stream and could fail the same way without end. Device changes
are followed as ao_wasapi_changenotify.c follows them (another eMultimedia
default when none was picked, the device removed or disabled, a new
PKEY_AudioEngine_DeviceFormat), and so are the renderer's own device events
when the session passes them on: those ask for a reload, which tries
passthrough again on whatever device is current.

mf.dll and mfplat.dll are loaded at runtime, so libmpv does not import them.

The IEC 61937 parser is plain C between "// --- bursts" markers, and
scripts/test_ao_mediafoundation_bursts.sh runs it on the host against FFmpeg's
spdif muxer.

